 {'tag': 'Heating',
 'col': '#D7262B',
 'ic': 'furnace',
 'date': 'Nov 2026',
 'iso': '2026-11-26',
 'slug': 'furnace-replacement-ontario-guide',
 't': 'Furnace Replacement in Ontario: What Affects the Cost',
 'x': 'Replacing a furnace in Ontario? What changes the price, how sizing works, permits, rebates and what install day looks like, from licensed HVAC technicians.',
 'body': ['Most people replace a furnace on short notice. The old one quits on a cold night, or a technician finds a cracked heat exchanger during a tune-up. Either way, you end up comparing quotes '
          'quickly. This guide explains what actually moves the price of a furnace replacement in Ontario, so you can read those quotes with confidence.',
          '## Why two quotes for the same house can look so different',
          'The furnace itself is only part of the job. Two contractors can quote the same brand and still be far apart, because one includes work the other leaves out. When you compare, check each '
          'quote for these items:',
          '- **Size and type of furnace.** A single-stage furnace costs less than a two-stage or modulating one. The modulating models run longer at low output, which keeps rooms more even and '
          'quieter.',
          '- **Venting.** Older mid-efficiency furnaces vent up a chimney. A new high-efficiency furnace vents through plastic pipe out the side or roof of the house. That pipe run, and where it can '
          'exit safely, changes the labour.',
          '- **Condensate drain.** High-efficiency furnaces make water. It needs a drain, and sometimes a small pump if the floor drain is far away.',
          '- **Ductwork and plenum.** The new cabinet rarely lines up exactly with the old ducts. Most installs need a new transition or plenum built from sheet metal. Badly undersized ducts may '
          'need more work.',
          "- **Air conditioner coil.** If you have central air, the indoor coil sits on top of the furnace. Sometimes it can be reused; sometimes it can't, especially with an older AC.",
          '- **Thermostat, gas line and electrical.** A two-stage or modulating furnace works best with a thermostat that can control it. Gas piping and power may also need updating to current code.',
          '- **Permits and removal.** Some cities ask for a permit for furnace replacement, and the old unit has to be hauled away.',
          'A low quote that skips venting, the plenum or the permit often grows on install day. Ask what is included in writing before you sign.',
          '## Getting the size right',
          'Bigger is not better. An oversized furnace heats the house in short bursts, shuts off, and starts again a few minutes later. That short-cycling wears parts, wastes gas and leaves cold '
          'spots between cycles.',
          'The right size comes from a heat loss calculation, not from the label on the old unit. Your old furnace may have been oversized from the start, and you may have added insulation or new '
          'windows since. A proper calculation looks at the size of each room, the windows, the insulation and the air leakage of the house. Ask any contractor how they sized your new furnace. If '
          'the answer is "same as the old one," ask again.',
          '## Efficiency: what new furnaces have to meet',
          'Every gas furnace manufactured for the Canadian market since July 3, 2019 must reach at least 95% AFUE, according to [Natural Resources '
          'Canada](https://natural-resources.canada.ca/energy-efficiency/energy-efficiency-regulations/gas-furnaces). In practice, the choice is no longer mid-efficiency versus high-efficiency. It '
          'is how the furnace runs: single-stage, two-stage or modulating, and what kind of blower motor it uses.',
          'For most Ontario homes, a two-stage furnace with a variable-speed blower is the sweet spot. It spends most of the winter on low fire, which is quieter and more even. A modulating furnace '
          'goes further and suits larger homes or houses with rooms that never seem to heat properly.',
          '## Should you look at a heat pump instead?',
          'If your furnace is old and your air conditioner is near the end too, it is worth pricing a cold-climate heat pump with a gas furnace as backup. The heat pump handles most of the heating '
          'and all of the cooling, and the furnace takes over on the coldest days.',
          "Canada's new National Heat Pump Rebate can pay up to $10,000 toward a qualifying heat pump installed from November 1, 2026, with applications expected to open in early 2027. See our "
          '[National Heat Pump Rebate page](national-heat-pump-rebate/) for the income limits and a calculator, and our [Ontario rebates page](rebates/) for the provincial program.',
          '## What install day looks like',
          '1. We protect the floors and shut off gas and power to the old furnace.',
          '2. The old unit comes out and the new one is set level on its pad.',
          '3. We build the sheet metal transition, connect the ducts and seal the joints.',
          '4. Venting, condensate drain, gas line and wiring are connected and tested.',
          '5. We start the furnace, check gas pressure and combustion, set the blower speed and walk you through the thermostat.',
          'Most straightforward replacements are done in a day. Changes to venting or ductwork can add time, and we tell you that in the quote.',
          "## After it's installed",
          'Register the warranty with the manufacturer. Many brands give a longer parts warranty only if you register within a set time. Change the filter on schedule and book a tune-up each fall. '
          'Keep the side-wall vent pipes clear of snow and ice in winter.',
          'If you live anywhere from the [GTA](service-areas/) to [Barrie](service-areas/barrie/), [Windsor](service-areas/windsor/) or [Kingston](service-areas/kingston/), we can visit, measure and '
          'give you a written quote with everything listed. Start with our [furnace page](furnaces/) or book a free in-home quote.'],
 'faq': [('How long does a furnace replacement take?', 'Most straightforward replacements are finished in one day. Venting or ductwork changes can add time, and a good quote says so up front.'),
         ('Can I keep my old air conditioner coil?', 'Sometimes. It depends on its size, condition and the refrigerant it uses. Your technician should check it before quoting.'),
         ('Do I need a permit to replace a furnace in Ontario?',
          "It depends on your city. Some cities list furnace replacement in their permit fees. Your contractor should know the local rule and apply when it's needed.")]},
 {'tag': 'Heating',
 'col': '#D7262B',
 'ic': 'furnace',
 'date': 'Nov 2026',
 'iso': '2026-11-26',
 'slug': 'signs-you-need-a-new-furnace',
 't': '7 Signs You Need a New Furnace: Ontario Homeowner Guide',
 'x': 'Old age, rising gas bills, repeat repairs, cold rooms or a CO alarm? How Ontario homeowners can tell when a furnace repair stops making sense.',
 'body': ['A furnace almost never dies out of nowhere. It usually drops hints for a winter or two first: a repair that comes back, a bedroom that stays cold, a gas bill that creeps up. The trick is '
          'reading those hints before the system quits on a January night in Brampton or Barrie, when every HVAC company in town is booked.',
          "Here are the seven signs we pay the most attention to when we're called out to an older furnace, and what each one usually means.",
          '## 1. The furnace is getting old',
          "Age on its own isn't a reason to rip out a furnace that runs well. But it changes the math on every repair. Look for the manufacture date on the rating plate inside the burner door, or in "
          'the serial number. Older mid-efficiency units with a metal chimney vent are often the ones nearing the end.',
          'On an older unit, ask two questions before paying for a big repair. Can the part still be found without a long wait? And will this be the last repair, or the first of several? If the '
          'honest answer to the second one is "probably not the last," put that money toward a new system instead.',
          '## 2. Gas bills keep going up',
          'Rates change, so compare your usage, not the dollar amount. Your gas bill shows how many cubic metres you used. If that number climbs from one winter to the next and nothing changed '
          'in the house, the system is likely working harder for the same heat. Dirty burners, a tired blower motor or a failing igniter can all do this.',
          "## 3. You're calling for repairs every winter",
          'One service call in ten years is normal. A new part every season is a pattern. Keep your invoices together. When the repairs on an older furnace start to add up to a real share of what a '
          'replacement costs, the repair is just buying time.',
          '## 4. Some rooms never get warm',
          'Cold bedrooms over a garage, a chilly back addition or a basement that feels like a cellar are common complaints in older Ontario homes. Sometimes the equipment is to blame. A blower losing '
          'speed or a dirty coil will cut airflow to the far rooms first.',
          "Often, though, the real cause is the duct system: undersized runs, crushed flex duct or leaky joints. That's worth knowing before you buy a new furnace, because a new unit on bad ducts "
          'will still leave the same rooms cold. We check both. If the ducts are the problem, our [ductwork and sheet metal](ductwork/) crew can fix the runs at the same time.',
          '## 5. Short-cycling',
          'Listen to how often the furnace starts. A healthy one runs a steady cycle and rests. If it fires, runs a few minutes, shuts off and starts again soon after, something is wrong. Common '
          'causes are a clogged filter, a thermostat in a bad spot, an overheating heat exchanger or a furnace that was too big for the house from the start.',
          'Oversizing is more common than people think. [Natural Resources Canada](https://prod-natural-resources.azure.cloud.nrcan-rncan.gc.ca/sites/nrcan/files/energy/pdf/energystar/Heating_With_Gas.pdf) advises sizing a '
          "furnace from a room-by-room heat loss calculation, not from floor area or the size of the old unit. If your current furnace was simply matched to the one before it, the next one shouldn't "
          'be.',
          '## 6. New noises',
          "Furnaces hum and click. They shouldn't bang, screech or rattle. A loud bang at start-up can mean delayed ignition, where gas builds up before it lights. Squealing usually points to the "
          "blower motor or its bearings. Rattling can be a loose panel, or it can be a crack spreading in the heat exchanger as the metal heats and cools. Don't guess with that one. Book a [furnace "
          'repair visit](hvac-repair/) and have it inspected.',
          '## 7. Signs of a combustion problem',
          "This is the one that can't wait. Watch for:",
          '- A burner flame that is lazy, yellow or orange instead of mostly blue',
          '- Soot or rust around the burner area or the vent pipe',
          '- Heavy condensation on the inside of windows near the furnace',
          '- Headaches, nausea or dizziness that ease when you leave the house',
          '- A carbon monoxide alarm going off',
          "If the CO alarm sounds, get everyone out right away and call 911 from outside, as the [Ontario government's CO safety page](https://www.ontario.ca/cosafety) advises. Since January 1, "
          '2026, the Ontario Fire Code requires a CO alarm on every storey of a house that has a fuel-burning appliance, fireplace or attached garage, as well as next to each sleeping area. If '
          "the basement has no alarm, that's a cheap fix to make this week.",
          'A cracked heat exchanger is the most common reason a technician will shut a furnace off and tag it. On an older unit, that crack usually means replacement rather than repair.',
          '## Repair or replace: how we help you decide',
          'When we inspect an older furnace, we give you both options in writing: what the repair costs and what it fixes, and what a new system would look like. We also tell you plainly if the '
          'furnace has years left in it. A well-kept unit with one bad part is often worth fixing.',
          'If you do replace, think about the whole system. If the central air is the same age as the furnace, pricing a [cold-climate heat pump](heat-pumps/) with a gas furnace as backup is worth a '
          'look. Federal help may apply, so check our [National Heat Pump Rebate page](national-heat-pump-rebate/) and our [Ontario rebates page](rebates/) for current programs.',
          '## Buy yourself time until then',
          '1. Change the filter. A clogged one causes more no-heat calls than almost anything else.',
          '2. Keep the side-wall intake and exhaust pipes clear of snow and ice after every storm.',
          "3. Don't close more than a couple of supply registers. It raises pressure and strains the blower.",
          '4. Book a yearly [furnace tune-up](hvac-maintenance/) in the fall, before the first cold snap.',
          "If you're seeing two or three of these signs, it's time for a straight answer. We cover the [GTA](service-areas/) and towns like [Oshawa](service-areas/oshawa/), "
          '[Hamilton](service-areas/hamilton/) and [Collingwood](service-areas/collingwood/). See our [furnace page](furnaces/) or [book a free quote](free-quote/) and a licensed technician will '
          'inspect it and give you the options in writing.'],
 'faq': [('How do I find out how old my furnace is?',
          'Open the burner door and look for the rating plate. It often shows a manufacture date. If not, the serial number usually encodes the year, and the maker or a technician can decode it for '
          'you.'),
         ('Is it safe to run a furnace with a cracked heat exchanger?',
          "No. A crack can let combustion gases, including carbon monoxide, into the air going through your house. A technician who finds one will usually shut the furnace off until it's replaced."),
         ('Do I need a carbon monoxide alarm on every floor in Ontario?',
          "Since January 1, 2026, yes, if your house has a fuel-burning appliance such as a gas furnace, a fireplace or an attached garage. You also need one next to each sleeping area. Ontario's CO "
          'safety page has the full rules.')]},
 {'tag': 'Rebates',
 'col': '#2E7D32',
 'ic': 'rebate',
 'date': 'Nov 2026',
 'iso': '2026-11-27',
 'slug': 'ontario-heat-pump-rebates',
 't': 'Ontario Heat Pump Rebates: What Your Home Can Actually Get',
 'x': 'How Ontario Home Renovation Savings heat pump rebates are worked out, why gas-heated homes get less, and how the new federal rebate fits in.',
 'body': ['Most ads say Ontario heat pump rebates go "up to $7,500." Some homes do qualify for that. Many homes in the GTA heat with natural gas, though, and they fall under a smaller rebate. Before '
          'you budget around a number, it helps to know which group your house is in and how the amount is worked out.',
          'This guide covers the provincial Home Renovation Savings Program and the new federal National Heat Pump Rebate, as their official pages read on November 27, 2026.',
          '## Who runs the Ontario program',
          'Home Renovation Savings is run by Save on Energy and Enbridge Gas. Heat pumps sit in the part of the program that needs no home energy assessment. The details are on the [official heat '
          'pump page](https://www.homerenovationsavings.ca/without-assessment/heat-pumps).',
          '## The rebate depends on how you heat now',
          'This is the part most people miss. The program pays per ton of heat pump capacity, and the rate depends on your main heat source today.',
          '- **Electricity, oil, propane or wood:** a cold-climate air-source heat pump earns $1,250 per ton, up to $7,500. A ground-source (geothermal) system earns $2,000 per ton, up to $12,000.',
          '- **Natural gas from Enbridge:** a cold-climate air-source heat pump earns $500 per ton, up to $2,000. A ground-source system gets a flat $3,000.',
          '- **Rented heat pumps:** they get the same amounts as the gas group, whatever fuel the home used before.',
          'Tons are counted from the rated heating capacity at 8.3°C (47°F), not from the cooling size printed on the box. So a 3-ton cold-climate unit in a house heated with electric baseboards '
          'would work out to $3,750. The same unit in a gas-heated house would work out to $1,500.',
          'That difference matters in places like Peterborough, Orillia or the cottage country north of Barrie, where plenty of homes still burn oil or propane or use electric baseboards. Those '
          'owners stand to gain the most from the provincial program.',
          '## Rules that can cost you the rebate',
          'The program is strict about order. Miss one of these and the rebate can be refused.',
          '1. **Pre-approval comes first.** The program says installs done before approval are not eligible. Plan the job before the old system fails, if you can.',
          '2. **Use a participating contractor.** The contractor sends the pre-installation and post-installation applications for you.',
          "3. **Pick a listed model.** The air-source unit must be an active model on Natural Resources Canada's qualified product list. Not every heat pump sold in Ontario is on it.",
          '4. **Own an eligible home.** Detached, semi-detached, row houses, townhomes and mobile homes on a permanent foundation qualify. Homes occupied for six months or less do not. If the home '
          'has tenants, the owner applies.',
          'Once the final application is approved, the program says the cheque should arrive by mail within 60 days.',
          '## The federal rebate on top',
          "Canada's [National Heat Pump Rebate](https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/campaigns/one-million-home-energy-retrofits.html) is a separate program run by the federal "
          'government. It covers heat pumps installed on or after November 1, 2026. The application portal is expected to open in early 2027.',
          '- Households with above-median income can get $2,000.',
          '- Low- to median-income households can get up to $10,000, capped at the cost of the install. Income limits depend on household size and province.',
          '- You apply yourself. Contractors cannot apply on your behalf.',
          '- The unit must be on the federal eligible product list by its AHRI number, as of the install date.',
          'Can you combine the two? The federal page says some households may be able to, with details to come before launch. Until those rules are published, nobody can promise you both. Our '
          '[National Heat Pump Rebate page](national-heat-pump-rebate/) has the income limits and a calculator, and we update it as the rules firm up.',
          '## Is a heat pump worth it in a gas-heated home?',
          'The federal page itself warns that switching off natural gas may not lower your heating bills, depending on where you live. In much of '
          'Ontario, gas is still cheap to burn.',
          'For gas homes, the setup that usually makes sense is a hybrid. A cold-climate heat pump does the cooling and most of the spring and fall heating. The gas furnace takes over on the coldest '
          'nights. If your central air is near the end of its life, that is the natural time to put in a heat pump instead of a new AC.',
          'For homes on oil, propane or electric baseboards, the case is much stronger. Running costs often drop, and the provincial rebate is at its highest.',
          '## Sizing still comes first',
          'A rebate is no reason to buy the wrong size. The federal program says the system must be able to heat the whole home, with more detail promised before launch. We size from a room-by-room '
          'heat loss calculation and check the ducts, since an older duct system can limit what a ducted heat pump delivers. Ductless units can work well in homes without ducts or for a cold '
          'addition.',
          '## What we do when we quote',
          '1. Check how your home is heated now, since that sets the provincial rate.',
          '2. Size the system and choose a model that appears on the lists for both programs.',
          '3. Show you each rebate separately, with the rate used, so you can see the math.',
          '4. Handle the provincial pre-approval before any work starts.',
          'See our [heat pump page](heat-pumps/) for the equipment we install, or our [Ontario rebates page](rebates/) for other upgrades such as insulation. We work across the [GTA](service-areas/) '
          'and in towns like [Peterborough](service-areas/peterborough/), [Orillia](service-areas/orillia/) and [Kingston](service-areas/kingston/). [Book a free quote](free-quote/) and we will tell '
          'you plainly which rebates your home qualifies for.'],
 'faq': [('Why is my heat pump rebate only $2,000 when ads say $7,500?',
          'Under Home Renovation Savings, homes that mainly heat with Enbridge natural gas get $500 per ton, up to $2,000. The $7,500 maximum is for homes heated with electricity, oil, propane or '
          'wood.'),
         ('Can I get the Ontario rebate if my heat pump is already installed?',
          'The provincial program requires pre-approval and says installs done before approval are not eligible. The federal National Heat Pump Rebate is different: it accepts installs from November '
          '1, 2026, with applications expected in early 2027.'),
         ('Do I need an energy assessment for a heat pump rebate?',
          'Not for the heat pump itself. The Ontario program lists heat pumps under upgrades that need no assessment, and the federal program says no pre- or post-installation audit is required.')]},
 {"tag": "Cooling", "col": "#1E1E1E", "ic": "ac", "date": "Sep 2026", "slug": "ac-sizing-guide",
  "t": "Why AC size matters more than brand", "x": "Oversized and undersized air conditioners both cost you comfort.",
  "body": ["An oversized air conditioner cools the house quickly, shuts off, and never runs long enough to remove humidity — so the home feels cold and clammy.",
           "An undersized unit runs all afternoon on hot days and still can't keep the upstairs comfortable.",
           "The right size comes from a cooling-load calculation that looks at your windows, insulation, orientation and ductwork — not the size of the old unit.",
           "We do that calculation for every new system we quote."]},
